From b85543d733e2eae8e2cae4dc706c66e98cb4c602 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 08:27:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Treat budget as overall spending capacity, not a
 per-Experience price ceiling
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Removed the hard budget_* checkbox filter that excluded an Experience outright if its price bracket didn't match the lead's selected tier -- it directly conflicted with the goal (an €850 Experience with a 1000-3000 budget was being excluded, even though it leaves room for Enhancements). Budget is now scoring-only.

computeBudgetRelevanceScore now uses calculateProposalTotal() (the same function already used for the real displayed price -- accounts for per_person pricing, guest tiers, seasonal overrides) instead of raw base_price, and rewards proximity to the requested tier asymmetrically: at or under the tier's ceiling is always full bonus (never penalized for leaving headroom to add Enhancements), over the ceiling the penalty grows continuously as a function of how far over relative to that tier's own width -- no fixed thresholds, self-calibrating per tier. Verified the exact scenarios and tolerance table from the spec (950/1050/1400/2500 against a 500-1000 budget, 850/2500 against 1000-3000, 850 against 200-500) with an isolated test -- all match the intended qualitative gradient (ottima/buona/bassa/incompatibile) and stay strictly monotonic.

No new architecture: reused the existing pricing utility, touched only generateProposal.ts. The admin budget_* checkboxes stay in the UI (out of scope) but no longer drive matching -- flagged to the user.
